  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 125 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper level ridge will allow for above average temps and \nsouthwesterly flow to persist across MT through Friday. Due to an \nupper level disturbance, there will be scattered showers and weak \nthunderstorms late this afternoon into the overnight hours, mainly\nimpacting SW MT. Additionally, high clouds will continue to \nspread northward as the day progresses. Warm temperatures will \npeak for most of MT on Thursday and Friday. Although temps will be\nabove average for this time of year, they will remain well below \nrecord levels. ESH\n\nA relatively strong Pacific cold front is forecast to \ntraverse the northern Rockies late Friday into Friday night. \nHowever, model discrepancies persist regarding the precise timing \nand intensity of the embedded shortwave as it lifts northeastward \nfrom the Northern Rockies into AB by Saturday morning. Gusty \nsouthwest winds are likely to precede the front late Friday with \nshowers and potentially some thunderstorms accompanying the \nfrontal passage, followed by gusty west winds and cooler \ntemperatures for the weekend. Hoenisch\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nDue to cape values remaining mainly under 500 J/kg, thunderstorm \nthreats will be primarily limited to isolated lightning strikes \nand brief gusty winds this afternoon and evening. \n\nLooking towards the weekend, the main concern will be stronger \nwinds along the Rocky Mountain Front and adjacent areas Friday \nafternoon through Saturday morning due to the approaching upper \nlevel trough and cold front. Strongest winds for the Rocky \nMountain Front are currently expected Friday night with a 30-40% \nchance of gusts 55 mph or above. During that same time frame, \nthere is a 10-20% of gusts exceeding 70 mph. ESH",
